    guide: [{ heading: "What take-home pay means", body: "Take-home pay is the cash that reaches your bank after taxes, retirement contributions, insurance premiums, and other deductions leave your gross salary. A $82,000 salary with $5,200 in retirement savings, $3,100 in insurance, and a 20% effective tax rate nets near $59,000 yearly, or about $4,917 monthly. Our salary calculator turns gross pay, one tax rate, and deductions into net annual and per-paycheck figures instantly, keeping every entry private offline." }, { heading: "How net pay is calculated", body: "The math is net equals gross minus pre-tax deductions minus tax on the remainder. For example, subtract $5,200 retirement and $3,100 insurance from $82,000 to get $73,700 taxable, then apply a 20% rate for $14,740 tax and $58,960 net. Divide by 12 for $4,913 monthly or by 26 for $2,268 biweekly. Raising pre-tax contributions lowers taxable income directly, so test $5,000 versus $8,000 deductions to see take-home shift." }, { heading: "Salary worked example: $80k gross is $60k net", body: "Enter $80,000 gross with a 20% rate and $5,000 deductions to see about $60,000 net, $5,000 monthly, and $2,308 biweekly. This single-rate model excludes progressive brackets, state tax, credits, and benefits rules, so real paychecks differ. For US withholding detail use the /us-paycheck-calculator tool, and for India FY 2026-27 CTC math use the /in-hand-salary-india tool. This is informational planning only, not tax advice. See /terms." }]
