    guide: [{ heading: "How SIP maturity is calculated", body: "Future value uses FV = M×((1+mr)^n−1)÷mr×(1+mr), where M is monthly investment, mr is annual÷12÷100, and n is months. For example, Rs 5,000 monthly at 12% for 10 years gives mr=0.01 and n=120, so Rs 6,00,000 invested grows to maturity near Rs 11,50,000 with gains near Rs 5,50,000. Presets clarify sensitivity: same Rs 5,000 for 10 years reaches near Rs 10,32,000 at 10% and near Rs 8,65,000 at 7%. Table — Horizon | Invested | Projected at 12%: 5yr | Rs 3,00,000 | Rs 4,12,000; 10yr | Rs 6,00,000 | Rs 11,50,000; 15yr | Rs 9,00,000 | Rs 24,97,000. Edge case: skipped instalments or mid-year pauses lower n and compounding rhythm. Limitation: fixed-rate maths excludes NAV volatility, expense ratios, exit loads, and taxation. Compare lump sums via /compound-interest-calculator and See /terms." }, { heading: "Inputs, assumptions and limitations", body: "Enter monthly SIP amount, expected annual return, and duration; projection assumes fixed monthly compounding on one debit date. A 10% yearly step-up dramatically beats flat SIPs because later, larger instalments still compound for years. Table — Plan | Total invested | Projected at 12% over 15 years: flat Rs 5,000 | Rs 9,00,000 | Rs 24,97,000; step-up Rs 5,000 rising 10% yearly | Rs 17,10,000 | Rs 52,90,000, about Rs 28,00,000 extra. SIP versus lumpsum contrast: Rs 6,00,000 one-time at 12% for 10 years reaches near Rs 18,60,000, while staggered Rs 5,000 monthly totals Rs 11,50,000 because early lump-sum months compound longest. Edge case: step-up pauses during job gaps revert maths toward flat trajectory. Limitation: salary growth, fund downtime, tracking error, and capital-gains slabs stay excluded. Stress-test 10% versus 12% beside /compound-interest-calculator and See /terms." }, { heading: "Next steps and common questions", body: "If maturity falls short, raise monthly amount, extend duration, or add yearly step-up to close gaps without chasing returns. For example, lifting Rs 5,000 to Rs 7,500 at 12% for 10 years moves maturity from near Rs 11,50,000 toward Rs 17,25,000, while stretching Rs 5,000 from 10 to 12 years moves near Rs 11,50,000 toward Rs 14,90,000. Revisit allocations yearly, rebalance equity-debt mix, automate debits just after salary, and keep emergency corpus separate covering six months essential expenses so redemptions never interrupt compounding. Edge case: stopping SIPs in crashes locks losses and forfeits recovery units. Limitation: projections ignore behavioural gaps, distributor commissions, fund mergers, and regulatory shifts. Model methodology at /methodology, confirm personal choices with qualified professional, compare /retirement-calculator goals, and see /terms before investing." }]
