    guide: [{ heading: "What CAGR means", body: "CAGR is the single steady yearly rate that grows a start value into an end value over whole years. $10,000 reaching $20,000 in 5 years means about 14.87% CAGR with a 2x multiple and $10,000 gain. Funds, stocks, SIPs, and revenue all use it for apples-to-apples comparison across years. Our CAGR calculator returns rate, multiple, and gain instantly, all private offline." }, { heading: "How CAGR, multiple, and gain are calculated", body: "CAGR equals (end÷start)^(1÷years)−1, multiple equals end÷start, and gain equals end−start. For example, $20,000 divided by $10,000 gives a 2x multiple and $10,000 gain, while ^(1÷5)−1 gives 14.87% yearly. A 3-year path to $18,000 yields about 21.6% but a smaller gain; compare end values and horizons side by side. Volatile yearly returns collapse into this one smoothed rate for fair comparison. Use the /compound-interest-calculator tool to project forward from any CAGR." }, { heading: "CAGR worked example: $10k to $20k in 5 years is 14.87 percent", body: "Enter $10,000 start, $20,000 end, 5 years to see about 14.87% CAGR, 2x multiple, and $10,000 gain, then try $18,000 end to feel the drop. This smooths volatility and excludes dividends, fees, taxes, and timing covered by the /sip-calculator tool. It is informational analysis only, not investment advice. Confirm choices with a qualified professional before investing. Copy results for reports and See /terms." }]
