    category: "finance",
    faq: [{ question: "Which compounding method does this FD calculator use?", answer: "It uses RBI-standard quarterly rests: A = P×(1+r/400)^(4×t), where r is the nominal annual rate and t is years. Effective annual yield exceeds the nominal rate because interest reinvests every quarter. For example, 7% nominal gives about 7.19% effective yield. See /terms." }, { question: "Is TDS included in the FD maturity?", answer: "No. Banks deduct TDS when yearly interest crosses Rs 40,000 (Rs 50,000 for seniors), but this tool shows pre-TDS maturity for illustration. For example, Rs 2,07,390 interest on a 5-year deposit faces TDS across years. Senior-citizen slabs differ, so confirm your bank notice and see /terms." }, { question: "Do senior-citizen rates or early withdrawal change maturity?", answer: "Yes in real banks but not in this projection: many Indian banks add 0.50% for seniors, while premature withdrawal costs a penalty plus a lower slab. For example, Rs 5,00,000 at 7% for 5 years matures near Rs 7,07,390, while the 7.50% senior rate reaches Rs 7,25,000. This shows pre-TDS compounding only; confirm your bank card rate and see /terms." }, { question: "Is my FD data private?", answer: "Yes. Your lump FD sum never leaves this phone. Tenure, rate and premature-exit math runs here offline with no upload and no sign-up. For example, a Rs 5,00,000 lump for 5 years stays private, needs no net, and clears on tab shut. Tool shows pre-TDS math only. See /terms." }, { question: "How should I ladder FDs for liquidity and yield?", answer: "Split Rs 5,00,000 into Rs 1,00,000 tranches maturing in one through five years, then roll each redemption into fresh five-year deposits. Laddering staggers cash availability while capturing rising card rates. Retain emergency tranches short, extend surplus tranches long, and avoid premature penalties. Reassess rate cycles annually. See /terms." }],
